course: dictionary
lesson: 14995
title: Repulsion: pushing away
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Repulsion is a force that pushes things away from each other. Think of two north poles of magnets: when you bring them close, they push apart. That push is repulsion.
explain: Repulsion can also be a feeling. «Mia felt repulsion when she saw the slimy worm in her sandwich.» Here repulsion means strong disgust, the feeling that makes you want to move away from something.
explain: Repulsion has an opposite: attraction, which pulls things together. Unlike poles of magnets attract, and like poles repel. When you meet repulsion in a text, ask: is something being pushed away, or is someone feeling disgusted?
fact: Repulsion means a force that pushes things away from each other.
fact: Like poles of magnets repel, and unlike poles attract.
fact: Repulsion can also mean a strong feeling of disgust.
fact: The verb is «repel», and the opposite of repulsion is attraction.
fact: In science lessons you will often see «attraction and repulsion» together.
exercise: What does repulsion mean in science? | A force that pulls things together | *A force that pushes things away from each other | A loud noise made by a magnet | A colour that glows
exercise: Which word means the opposite of repulsion, a pull towards something? | Heaviness | Repulsion | *Attraction | Disgust
exercise: «Mia felt repulsion when she saw the slimy worm in her sandwich.» What does repulsion mean here? | A tired feeling after a long walk | *Strong disgust | Happy excitement
test: Two like poles of magnets are placed together. What happens? | They join to make one bigger magnet | They stick together forever | They attract each other | *They repel each other and push apart
test: «Tom's face showed repulsion when he tasted the sour milk.» What feeling does repulsion show here? | Surprise in a good way | Excitement | *Disgust | Sleepiness
exam: Which sentence uses repulsion correctly to mean a push-apart force? | Repulsion is the word for a bright, sunny day. | Mia showed repulsion by singing happily at the party. | *When the like poles of two magnets came close, they showed repulsion. | The bus had repulsion and left late.
